const articleHtml = "<p>Biosecurity is the set of practices that reduce the chance of a pathogen entering a bird collection and spreading once it is there. Testing is useful, but it is not the whole system.</p>\n<p>A collection can have a perfect test report and still have a biosecurity problem if new birds share airspace, equipment moves between rooms, or visitors handle birds without washing their hands.</p>\n<h2>What Is Avian Biosecurity?</h2>\n<p>Avian biosecurity is a management plan built around three questions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>How do we keep pathogens out?</li>\n<li>How do we stop them from spreading inside?</li>\n<li>How do we know whether the plan is working?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The answers include physical separation, traffic control, cleaning, health records, and targeted testing.</p>\n<h2>The Three Core Components</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Layer</th>\n<th>What it covers</th>\n<th>Practical examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Isolation</td>\n<td>Keeping new, sick, or suspect birds away from the main group</td>\n<td>Separate room, separate airspace, dedicated equipment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Traffic control</td>\n<td>Controlling who and what moves between areas</td>\n<td>Footwear changes, hand hygiene, visitor limits, equipment logs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sanitation</td>\n<td>Reducing contamination on surfaces, tools, and hands</td>\n<td>Cleaning, disinfection, drying, waste management</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Testing supports these layers. It does not replace them.</p>\n<h2>How Disease Testing Supports Biosecurity</h2>\n<p>Testing can:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>identify a pathogen in a new bird before introduction;</li>\n<li>establish a baseline for a group;</li>\n<li>help investigate an outbreak;</li>\n<li>support a decision to isolate or retest;</li>\n<li>provide a record of health status over time.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Testing cannot:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>prove a bird is free of every pathogen;</li>\n<li>remove contamination from a surface;</li>\n<li>replace quarantine;</li>\n<li>decide treatment for a veterinarian.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Managing New and Returning Birds</h2>\n<p>New arrivals and birds returning from shows, races, or sales should be treated as separate risk events.</p>\n<p>A workable process:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>assess the source and travel history;</li>\n<li>examine the bird;</li>\n<li>decide whether targeted testing is warranted;</li>\n<li>quarantine in a separate area;</li>\n<li>monitor for signs;</li>\n<li>review the results and veterinary advice before introduction.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A negative test from the seller is context, not a clearance.</p>\n<h2>Separating Healthy, Quarantined and Sick Birds</h2>\n<p>A simple three-zone model helps:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Main collection:</strong> healthy, stable birds.</li>\n<li><strong>Quarantine:</strong> new or returning birds with no current signs.</li>\n<li><strong>Isolation:</strong> birds with signs or a positive result that requires separation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The zones should have separate equipment and a defined traffic order. Handle the main collection first, then quarantine, then isolation, or follow the veterinarian's advice.</p>\n<h2>Cleaning and Disinfection</h2>\n<p>Cleaning removes organic material; disinfection reduces viable pathogens. A surface that is not cleaned first may not be effectively disinfected.</p>\n<p>Key practices:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>remove droppings, feed, and bedding before disinfection;</li>\n<li>use a disinfectant appropriate for the surface and pathogen;</li>\n<li>allow the correct contact time;</li>\n<li>clean water containers and utensils separately;</li>\n<li>dry surfaces where possible;</li>\n<li>discard or disinfect porous materials that cannot be cleaned.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Personnel, Equipment and Visitor Management</h2>\n<p>People and equipment move pathogens as easily as birds.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep dedicated clothing or footwear for each area.</li>\n<li>Wash hands before and after handling birds.</li>\n<li>Clean and disinfect shared tools between groups.</li>\n<li>Limit visitors and record who enters the bird areas.</li>\n<li>Do not let visitors handle birds without a hygiene protocol.</li>\n<li>Keep food, medication, and clean supplies away from dirty areas.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Maintaining Bird Health Records</h2>\n<p>A record system does not need to be complicated. It needs to be consistent.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Record</th>\n<th>Why it matters</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Bird ID, species, origin</td>\n<td>Identifies the individual and its exposure history</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Arrival and quarantine dates</td>\n<td>Shows separation and timing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Veterinary examinations</td>\n<td>Records clinical findings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tests, samples, results</td>\n<td>Tracks the evidence trail</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Treatments or interventions</td>\n<td>Provides context for later results</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Movements between groups</td>\n<td>Helps trace contacts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cleaning and disinfection log</td>\n<td>Shows whether the plan was followed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Records are especially useful when a result is unexpected or when a new bird's history is unclear.</p>\n<h2>Creating a Monitoring System</h2>\n<p>A monitoring system should include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a schedule of observations and tests;</li>\n<li>a named person responsible for each task;</li>\n<li>a defined response to abnormal signs;</li>\n<li>a review of results over time;</li>\n<li>a plan for updating the protocol.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The system should be practical enough to follow on a busy day. A protocol that only works when nothing else is happening is not a biosecurity system.</p>\n<h2>Reviewing Biosecurity Performance</h2>\n<p>Review the plan after:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a new bird introduction;</li>\n<li>a positive result;</li>\n<li>an outbreak;</li>\n<li>a change in housing or staff;</li>\n<li>a show or race season.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ask what worked, what was skipped, and what should change. A biosecurity plan is a living document, not a certificate on the wall.</p>\n<h2>Laboratory Perspective</h2>\n<p>SENO provides avian molecular testing for selected targets. This article describes a biosecurity framework, not a veterinary diagnosis or a guarantee that a collection is free of disease. The current testing menu and sample requirements should be confirmed with the laboratory. Testing is most useful when it supports isolation, sanitation, and a documented management plan.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<p><strong>Can testing replace quarantine?</strong></p>\n<p>No. Testing samples a moment in time. Quarantine provides physical separation while uncertainty is managed.</p>\n<p><strong>What is the most important biosecurity practice?</strong></p>\n<p>There is no single practice. Isolation, hand hygiene, traffic control, and cleaning work together. The weakest link is often the one that is skipped under time pressure.</p>\n<p><strong>How often should equipment be cleaned?</strong></p>\n<p>Follow the manufacturer's instructions and the facility's risk plan. Shared tools and high-contact surfaces usually need more attention than low-contact storage areas.</p>\n<p><strong>Do I need records if I only keep a few birds?</strong></p>\n<p>Yes. Even a small collection benefits from a simple record of arrivals, signs, tests, and treatments. It makes pattern recognition possible.</p>\n<p><strong>Can a negative test allow a bird to skip isolation?</strong></p>\n<p>No. A negative result covers the sample and target tested. Isolation and observation remain separate safeguards.</p>\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>USDA APHIS — Defend the Flock: Biosecurity. https://www.aphis.usda.gov/livestock-poultry-disease/avian/defend-the-flock</li>\n<li>CDC — Psittacosis: About. https://www.cdc.gov/psittacosis/about/index.html</li>\n<li>CDC — Bird flu: About. https://www.cdc.gov/bird-flu/about/index.html</li>\n<li>Merck Veterinary Manual — Pet birds. https://www.merckvetmanual.com/exotic-and-laboratory-animals/pet-birds</li>\n<li>WOAH — Avian influenza. https://www.woah.org/en/disease/avian-influenza/</li>\n<li>AAVLD — Accreditation requirements. https://www.aavld.org/accreditation-requirements</li>\n</ol>";
